IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cica-dates`
IMPORT `cica-victim`

-- Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003 (WA) -- worked cases, from the seat of the victim (and, where the victim died,
-- the close relative for whom the personal representative claims).
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (mrdoc_47649), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the
-- facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every opinion or satisfaction of an assessor is given as a fact.
--
-- A breached or residual DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the s 9 and s 55 rules are asserted
-- through CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and one residual of each is #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.

-- HYPOTHETICAL: Sam is punched outside a nightclub in Perth on 14 March 2024. The 3 years of s 9(1) end on 14 March 2027.
`Sam was punched` MEANS `the day` 14 3 2024
`any date` MEANS `the day` 1 1 2025
`victim` MEANS `the person injured by the offence (the victim)`
`representative` MEANS `the personal representative of a person who died as a consequence of the offence`
`convicted` MEANS `someone was convicted of the offence (including a spent conviction, a finding of guilt with no conviction recorded, or being dealt with by a juvenile justice team)`
`single` MEANS `a single offence (even if committed by two or more people acting in concert)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 4: am I a close relative.
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `parent` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `grandparent` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `stepparent` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `spouse` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was the spouse or a de facto partner of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `de facto partner` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was the spouse or a de facto partner of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `child` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `grandchild` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `stepchild` TRUE EQUALS `yes: I was a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(c))`
-- A sister is not a close relative (unlike the Fatal Accidents Act 1959, which lists brothers and sisters).
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `brother or sister` TRUE EQUALS `no: section 4(1) does not list my relationship; brothers, sisters and other relatives are not close relatives`
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)` TRUE EQUALS `no: section 4(1) does not list my relationship; brothers, sisters and other relatives are not close relatives`
-- Married the victim after the offence: not "immediately before".
#ASSERT `Am I a close relative question` `spouse` FALSE EQUALS `no: the relationship must have existed immediately before the offence was committed (s 4(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 8, 12-17: can I apply. Arguments: applying as, date of offence, criminal case, claim someone else did it, charged
-- again, the victim suffered injury, a close relative suffered loss.
-- Sam: the offender is convicted; s 12.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `convicted` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 12 (proved offence)`)
-- s 8: an offence on 21 January 1971 is outside the Act; one on 22 January 1971 is inside it.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` (`the day` 21 1 1971) `convicted` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` (`the day` 22 1 1971) `convicted` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 12 (proved offence)`)
-- The charge is still before the court.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `a charge for the offence has been laid but not yet decided` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `while a charge for the offence is still undecided, none of sections 12 to 17 yet applies; the route depends on how the charge ends (the CEO may ask for the application to be stayed meanwhile, s 22)`
-- Acquittal: s 13 only if Sam claims someone else did it.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `the person charged was found not guilty, other than on account of mental impairment` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 13 (alleged offence: acquittal)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `the person charged was found not guilty, other than on account of mental impairment` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: after an acquittal, an application lies only if the applicant claims the offence was committed by someone other than the person acquitted (s 13(2)(b), (3)(c))`
-- The same for a personal representative (s 13(3)(c)).
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `representative` `Sam was punched` `the person charged was found not guilty, other than on account of mental impairment` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: after an acquittal, an application lies only if the applicant claims the offence was committed by someone other than the person acquitted (s 13(2)(b), (3)(c))`
-- Mental impairment: s 14, with no claim about who did it.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `the person charged was found not guilty on account of mental impairment` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 14 (alleged offence: acquittal due to mental impairment)`)
-- Unfit to stand trial: s 15 for an offence on or after 1 January 1986 (the first day); s 16 for one on 31 December 1985.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` (`the day` 1 1 1986) `the person charged was found unfit to stand trial` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 15 (alleged offence: accused unfit to stand trial)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` (`the day` 31 12 1985) `the person charged was found unfit to stand trial` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 16 (alleged offence: charge not determined)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` (`the day` 31 12 1985) `the person charged was found unfit to stand trial` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `the person charged has been charged again with the offence or tried for it, so section 16 does not apply (s 16(1)(f)); the route depends on how that charge ends`
-- Charge withdrawn: s 16, unless the person has been charged again.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `the charge was withdrawn or discontinued, dismissed without a finding, met with no evidence from the prosecutor, or the person charged died or was otherwise not brought to trial` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 16 (alleged offence: charge not determined)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `the charge was withdrawn or discontinued, dismissed without a finding, met with no evidence from the prosecutor, or the person charged died or was otherwise not brought to trial` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `the person charged has been charged again with the offence or tried for it, so section 16 does not apply (s 16(1)(f)); the route depends on how that charge ends`
-- No one charged: s 17.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `no one has been charged with the offence` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `section 17 (alleged offence: no person charged)`)
-- No injury at all (only property damaged).
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `victim` `Sam was punched` `convicted` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `sections 12 to 17 give a right to apply to a person who suffers injury (bodily harm, mental and nervous shock, or pregnancy); this Act does not speak to a person who suffered none`
-- Lee is killed; his executor applies for his wife's loss; the offender is convicted.
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `representative` `Sam was punched` `convicted` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: as the personal representative I may apply for compensation for the loss the close relatives suffered as a result of the death` `section 12 (proved offence)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `representative` `Sam was punched` `no one has been charged with the offence` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: as the personal representative I may apply for compensation for the loss the close relatives suffered as a result of the death` `section 17 (alleged offence: no person charged)`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply question` `representative` `Sam was punched` `convicted` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the personal representative may apply only if a close relative of the deceased suffered loss as a result of the death`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 11: how do I apply.
#ASSERT `How do I apply question` 30 FALSE EQUALS `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor (s 11(1))`
#ASSERT `How do I apply question` 18 FALSE EQUALS `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor (s 11(1))`
#ASSERT `How do I apply question` 17 FALSE EQUALS `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(a))`
#ASSERT `How do I apply question` 40 TRUE EQUALS `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am a represented person, my guardian or administrator may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(b))`
#ASSERT `How do I apply question` 16 TRUE EQUALS `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18 and a represented person, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent, or my guardian or administrator, may make it for me (s 11(1), (2))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 9: by when must I apply. Sam's 3 years end on 14 March 2027.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` `Sam was punched` (`the day` 1 6 2024) EQUALS `in time: the application must be made by the last day of the 3 years (s 9(1))` (`the day` 14 3 2027)
-- The last day.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` `Sam was punched` (`the day` 14 3 2027) EQUALS `in time: the application must be made by the last day of the 3 years (s 9(1))` (`the day` 14 3 2027)
-- A day late.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` `Sam was punched` (`the day` 15 3 2027) EQUALS `late: the 3 years have ended; an assessor may allow the application if he or she thinks it just, on any conditions he or she thinks just to impose (s 9(2))` (`the day` 14 3 2027)
-- An offence on 29 February 2024: the 3 years end on 28 February 2027.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` (`the day` 29 2 2024) (`the day` 28 2 2027) EQUALS `in time: the application must be made by the last day of the 3 years (s 9(1))` (`the day` 28 2 2027)
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` (`the day` 29 2 2024) (`the day` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `late: the 3 years have ended; an assessor may allow the application if he or she thinks it just, on any conditions he or she thinks just to impose (s 9(2))` (`the day` 28 2 2027)
-- A child abused in 2010 who applies as an adult in 2026: late; only the assessor's discretion can let it in.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` (`the day` 1 7 2010) (`the day` 1 7 2026) EQUALS `late: the 3 years have ended; an assessor may allow the application if he or she thinks it just, on any conditions he or she thinks just to impose (s 9(2))` (`the day` 1 7 2013)
#ASSERT `By when must I apply question` (`the day` 1 1 1970) `any date` EQUALS `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`

-- The s 9 rule: day 0 is 14 March 2024; 14 March 2027 is day 1095 (365 x 3: no 29 February falls in between).
`Sam applies on the last day` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for an offence on` `Sam was punched`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, the applicant` `make the compensation application` 1095))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `Sam applies on the last day` EQUALS TRUE
`Sam applies a day late` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for an offence on` `Sam was punched`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, the applicant` `make the compensation application` 1096))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `Sam applies a day late` EQUALS FALSE
`Sam applies late and an assessor allows it` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for an offence on` `Sam was punched`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, the applicant` `make the compensation application` 1096), (EVENT `an assessor` `allow the application to be made after the 3 years` 1200))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `Sam applies late and an assessor allows it` EQUALS TRUE
#TRACE `the s 9 rule for an offence on` `Sam was punched` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `I, the applicant` DOES `make the compensation application` AT 1096
-- expect: not BREACH: the residual permission, PARTY an assessor MAY allow the application to be made after the 3 years

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 10: the entitlement ends on death.
#ASSERT `Does the entitlement survive question` `the injured victim's own entitlement` FALSE EQUALS `yes: the person entitled is alive, so section 10 does not end the entitlement`
-- Sam dies of an unrelated illness before the award: the claim ends with him.
#ASSERT `Does the entitlement survive question` `the injured victim's own entitlement` TRUE EQUALS `no: the injured victim's entitlement ceased on the victim's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(1), (3))`
#ASSERT `Does the entitlement survive question` `a close relative's entitlement for loss from the victim's death` TRUE EQUALS `no: the close relative's entitlement ceased on the close relative's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(2), (3))`

