-- ss 31-34: the most I can be awarded. Arguments: date of the (last) offence, indictable, the offences, date applied.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` `Sam was punched` TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2024) EQUALS `answered` 75000
-- Item 6 begins on the day the Act came into operation (1 January 2004); item 5 ends the day before.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 1 2004) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 75000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 31 12 2003) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 50000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 7 1991) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 50000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 30 6 1991) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 20000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 1 1986) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 20000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 31 12 1985) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 15000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 1 1983) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 15000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 31 12 1982) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 7500
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 18 10 1976) FALSE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 7500
-- Item 1: indictable $2 000, simple $300.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 17 10 1976) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 2000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 17 10 1976) FALSE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 300
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 22 1 1971) FALSE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `answered` 300
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 21 1 1971) TRUE `single` (`the day` 1 6 2004) EQUALS `not covered` `the Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8), so it sets no maximum`
-- s 33(2): related offences, the last in 2024: $75 000 in aggregate.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` `Sam was punched` TRUE `two or more related offences (as an assessor is satisfied: committed at about the same time, or related for another reason)` (`the day` 1 6 2024) EQUALS `answered` 75000
-- s 33(3): two of my close relatives killed in related offences: twice.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` `Sam was punched` TRUE `two or more related offences from which two or more of my close relatives died, and I claim as a result of two or more of those deaths` (`the day` 1 6 2024) EQUALS `answered` 150000
-- s 34: unrelated offences by one offender: twice the maximum for the last; from 23 September 2003 (the first day).
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` `Sam was punched` TRUE `two or more offences committed by one person that are not related` (`the day` 1 6 2024) EQUALS `answered` 150000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 9 2003) TRUE `two or more offences committed by one person that are not related` (`the day` 23 9 2003) EQUALS `answered` 100000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded question` (`the day` 1 9 2003) TRUE `two or more offences committed by one person that are not related` (`the day` 22 9 2003) EQUALS `not covered` `section 34 applies to an application made on or after 23 September 2003; for an earlier application this Act does not say what the maximum is for unrelated offences by one offender`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 35: mental and nervous shock. Arguments: date applied, (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), s 35(3).
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 22 9 2003) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 does not stand in the way: it applies to applications made on or after 23 September 2003 (s 35(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 23 9 2003) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an award for mental and nervous shock needs one of the links in section 35(2), and none is met`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 allows it: I also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant (s 35(2)(a))`
-- A burglary while the owner was home: the offence was against her property.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 allows it: the offence was committed against me or my property (s 35(2)(b))`
-- A bystander who saw a stabbing.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 allows it: someone else died or was injured and I was personally present when or immediately after the offence was committed (s 35(2)(c))`
-- A mother whose adult son, living elsewhere, was killed.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 allows it: I was the parent or stepparent of a person who died from the offence (s 35(2)(d))`
-- A wife living with her husband, who was injured.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 35 allows it: I was a close relative of a person injured or killed by the offence and living with them (s 35(2)(e))`
-- An adult son who lived elsewhere and was not present when his father was injured: none of the links.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an award for mental and nervous shock needs one of the links in section 35(2), and none is met`
-- s 35(3) prevails even over (d): the son was killed while committing a separate offence.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for shock question` (`the day` 1 6 2024) FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the person who died or was injured was committing a separate offence, and no award can be made to a close relative for mental and nervous shock from that death or injury (s 35(3))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 36-41: what stops an award. Arguments: applying as, date of offence, s 36, motor vehicle caused, vehicle used to
-- commit, offence is a crime, s 38, separate offence, s 40, s 41.
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 36: because of a relationship or connection between the offender and the victim or close relative, money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the offender`)
-- s 37: a drink driver injures Sam (a simple offence, the car not used to commit it): barred.
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 37: the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle, and the vehicle was not used to commit an offence that is a crime`)
-- The car deliberately driven at Sam (a crime): not barred.
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
-- Used to commit the offence, but the offence is not a crime: barred ((a) and (b) are both needed).
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 37: the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle, and the vehicle was not used to commit an offence that is a crime`)
-- s 37(2): offences from 1 July 1993 (the first day); 30 June 1993 is outside it.
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` (`the day` 1 7 1993) FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 37: the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle, and the vehicle was not used to commit an offence that is a crime`)
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` (`the day` 30 6 1993) FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 38: the victim or close relative did not do something they ought reasonably to have done to help identify, apprehend or prosecute the offender`)
-- s 39: the injured victim (subsection (1)); the relative of a victim killed (subsection (2)).
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 39(1): I was injured when I was committing a separate offence`)
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `representative` `Sam was punched` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 39(2): the person who died was committing a separate offence when they died`)
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS (LIST `s 41: the victim's behaviour, condition, attitude or disposition contributed to the injury or death, and an assessor thinks it just to refuse an award because of it`)
-- Two bars together, in the Act's order.
#ASSERT `What stops an award question` `victim` `Sam was punched` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS (LIST `s 36: because of a relationship or connection between the offender and the victim or close relative, money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the offender`, `s 40: an award has already been made, or an application refused, for this injury or loss`)

