-- ss 19(5), 63(5), 64(5), 70, 71: what I face. False information alleged on 1 March 2025: prosecution by 1 March 2027.
#ASSERT `What do I face question` `giving information I knew was false in a material particular (s 70(2))` (`the day` 1 3 2025) (`the day` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount and an order to repay any compensation paid on the application; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (ss 70, 71)` 5000 (`the day` 1 3 2027)
#ASSERT `What do I face question` `giving information I knew was false in a material particular (s 70(2))` (`the day` 1 3 2025) (`the day` 2 3 2027) EQUALS `no prosecution: the 2 years for commencing one have ended (s 71)` (`the day` 1 3 2027)
#ASSERT `What do I face question` `not complying with an assessor's notice to give information, appear or produce a record (s 19(5))` (`the day` 1 3 2025) (`the day` 1 6 2025) EQUALS `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (s 71)` 5000 (`the day` 1 3 2027)
#ASSERT `What do I face question` `publishing in breach of an assessor's order restricting publication (s 64(5))` (`the day` 1 3 2025) (`the day` 1 6 2025) EQUALS `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (s 71)` 5000 (`the day` 1 3 2027)
#ASSERT `What do I face question` `as a witness, not appearing, not taking an oath or affirmation, or not giving evidence or producing a record (s 63(5))` (`the day` 1 3 2025) (`the day` 1 6 2025) EQUALS `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (s 71)` 5000 (`the day` 1 3 2027)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 6, 30: what can I be compensated for. Arguments: applying as, what for, the item, worn when injured.
`any item` MEANS `clothing or footwear`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `the injury itself (for example pain and suffering, or loss of enjoyment of life)` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: compensation may be awarded for the injury itself, as much as the assessor is satisfied is just (s 30(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `expenses paid because of the injury (for example medical bills, travel to treatment)` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the expenses were actually and reasonably incurred and arise directly from the injury (s 6(2)(a)(i))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `the cost of a report about the injury from a health professional or a counsellor` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the expense was actually and reasonably incurred (s 6(2)(a)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `treatment I am likely to need in future because of the injury` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the expenses are likely to be reasonably incurred for treatment I am likely to need as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(b)); the amount is paid only on a request made in time under section 48`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `earnings I lost because of the injury` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the earnings were lost as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(c))`
-- Sam's glasses, broken by the punch while he wore them: a personal item.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured` `spectacles or contact lenses` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the damage was a direct consequence of the offence: the item is a personal item and I was wearing it when injured (s 6(1), (2)(d))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured` `a surgical appliance or implant` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes, if the damage was a direct consequence of the offence: the item is a personal item and I was wearing it when injured (s 6(1), (2)(d))`
-- His jacket, left on a chair and slashed: not worn.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured` `clothing or footwear` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: damage to a personal item counts only if I was wearing it when I was injured (s 6(2)(d))`
-- His watch, worn and smashed: only if the regulations prescribe it.
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured` `some other item (for example a watch, jewellery or a phone)` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `section 6(1)(g) lets regulations prescribe further personal items, and the regulations are not deposited`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `loss of or damage to other property (for example a phone in my bag, cash taken, my car)` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: property other than a personal item I was wearing is not "loss" under section 6(2)`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `victim` `a close relative's loss from the victim's death` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the injured victim's claim is for the injury and the victim's own loss; a close relative's loss from a death is claimed by the deceased's personal representative (s 30(1), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `representative` `a close relative's loss from the victim's death` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, for a close relative's loss of a kind for which damages could be awarded under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (s 6(3), s 30(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I be compensated for it question` `representative` `the injury itself (for example pain and suffering, or loss of enjoyment of life)` `any item` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: on a death, the personal representative's claim is for the close relatives' loss from the death (s 6(3), s 30(3))`
